{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"resume","trigger":"plausible","reason":"On resume, redaction can change saved executable configuration or phase state, causing checks to fail or stages to be skipped."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A credential matching part of the startup marker causes successful sandboxed gates to be rejected, blocking runs and landing."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Repeated output matching a self-overlapping credential can cause unbounded buffering and scanning, potentially exhausting resources or stalling the daemon."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An unrelated object with matching fields gains a fabricated confinement verdict in API or artifact output."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Downloading an artifact containing meaningful terminal escape bytes can return altered content, as reproduced with a patch."},{"id":"C6","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A field containing the redaction marker is entirely withheld from MCP or digest output, hiding useful information."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A multiline credential split across stdout and stderr can be reconstructed after per-stream redaction and stored or logged."},{"id":"C8","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A fresh deployment process leaves the credential registry empty, allowing a failing gate to expose a provider credential in its error."},{"id":"C9","consequence":"diag","trigger":"normal","reason":"The unused optional metadata adds surface but the evidence establishes no runtime failure."},{"id":"C4b","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Normalizing every string can remove meaningful escape bytes from a patch or request and change the data presented to users or models."}]}